{
  "abstract": "We bound the number of original Riemann zeta zeros to the right of a prescribed line in every short interval, counting multiplicities and both height endpoints. Let $I=[U,U+H]$ with $T\\le U\\le2T$, and put $d=\\sigma-1/2$. Uniformly for $0\\le d<1/2$ and $T^{\\eta_0}\\le H\\le T$, the count is $O_{\\eta_0}((\\log T)H^{(1-2d)/(1-(2-p)d)})$, with $p=1$ for $\\eta_0>621/2000$ and $p=4/3$ for $\\eta_0>135/167$. A dyadic coefficient estimate saves a further factor $[1+(1-2d)\\log H]^{pd/(1-(2-p)d)}$. At fixed abscissa the fourth-moment case gives Ingham's power with a smaller logarithmic prefactor in the corresponding window range. Near the line the count at scaled depth $D$ is $O_{\\eta_0}(H\\log T\\exp(-pD\\log H/\\log T))$. This linear depth rate also holds for windows up to any fixed power of the reference height. The proof uses an unshifted spectral divisor estimate for the fourth-moment error, with a separate continuation of the arithmetic main terms. It starts from a bounded mollified moment on the critical line and constructs an inverse through length $H$, cancelling the initial Dirichlet coefficients exactly. A sharp projection on a finite prime torus supplies coefficient energy for the inverse and the residual. An interior cutoff combines that energy with the critical-line moment, and Littlewood's lemma converts the resulting estimate into a zero count. A geometric companion gives the local Density Hypothesis exponent for base copies whose selected depth rectangles satisfy a covering budget. The size of the complementary family remains open. For $H\\le T$, the sum of $H^{p|\\beta-1/2|}$ over all original zero copies in the window is $O_{p,\\eta_0}(H\\log T)$ in the corresponding ranges. The supplement gives the moment calculations, the cutoff and pole estimates, the geometric proof and deterministic CPU checks.",
